<Project>

  <!--
    Stipulate's build integration: everything a project referencing the Stipulate package gets,
    beyond the analyzer and code fix (analyzers/) and Stipulate.Contracts (lib/). This repository
    imports this same file (Directory.Build.targets), so its own builds exercise what ships.

    It is in buildTransitive/, so it reaches every project that gets Stipulate, directly or through
    another project or package: a class there that implements an interface from a library using
    Stipulate is held to its contracts like any other. StipulateTransitive=false leaves the projects
    that don't reference Stipulate themselves with Stipulate.Contracts alone, as an ordinary library.

    It is a .targets file, imported after the project's own properties, so a project can set
    StipulateMode and the other properties below in its .csproj as well as in a
    Directory.Build.props or on the command line.

    StipulateMode chooses how the validation attributes are enforced at run time:

      Weaving       (default) After the project compiles, the weaver rewrites its assembly so that
                    every method with validated parameters checks them on entry.
      Interceptors  The generator intercepts each call to such a method at compile time.
      None          No enforcement; the analyzer still checks contracts.

    Stipulate is for C# above all: the analyzer, the code fix and the generator are built for C#'s
    compiler (NuGet gives them only to C# projects). Weaving works in Visual Basic and F# projects
    too, with the check in their language. Interceptors, a C# feature, don't, nor does anything in
    a project in another language: such a project gets Stipulate.Contracts alone, and a warning
    (ST0008) unless its StipulateMode is None.

    StipulateFSharpChecks chooses, in an F# project that is woven, which calls the checks are
    guaranteed on. F#'s optimizer, unlike C#'s and Visual Basic's compilers, copies a small method's
    body into its callers before the weaver adds the checks to the method, so such a call isn't
    checked:

      EveryCall                  (default) Every call: F#'s optimizer inlines nothing in this
                                 project (fsc's localoptimize- switch; the JIT still optimizes),
                                 and offers nothing to other assemblies to inline (its
                                 nooptimizationdata switch).
      CallsFromOtherAssemblies   Calls from other assemblies: F#'s optimizer works as usual within
                                 the project, but offers nothing to other assemblies to inline.
      NoInliningMethods          Only calls to methods marked [<MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.NoInlining)>],
                                 which F# never inlines: F#'s optimizer works as usual.

    In every setting, a method marked NoInlining is checked on every call, and an F# inline member
    is never checked when F# calls it: F# always copies it into its callers.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <StipulateMode Condition="'$(StipulateMode)' == ''">Weaving</StipulateMode>
    <StipulateTransitive Condition="'$(StipulateTransitive)' == ''">true</StipulateTransitive>
    <StipulateFSharpChecks Condition="'$(StipulateFSharpChecks)' == ''">EveryCall</StipulateFSharpChecks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Assemblies whose files are under StipulateSolutionRoot count as part of the solution: an
    attribute declared in one that can't be compared is reported as ST2001 ("describe it") rather
    than ST2002 ("it's someone else's; describe it from your assembly"). By default, the solution's
    folder when building one, otherwise the project's own.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(StipulateSolutionRoot)' == ''">
    <StipulateSolutionRoot Condition="'$(SolutionDir)' != '' And '$(SolutionDir)' != '*Undefined*'">$(SolutionDir)</StipulateSolutionRoot>
    <StipulateSolutionRoot Condition="'$(StipulateSolutionRoot)' == ''">$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)</StipulateSolutionRoot>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <CompilerVisibleProperty Include="StipulateSolutionRoot" />
    <!--
      A change of mode must recompile: the assembly in obj\ was woven, or compiled with the
      interceptors, and nothing a change of mode touches is newer than it. MSBuild hashes these
      items into CoreCompileInputs.cache, which recompiles whenever the hash changes. So must a
      change of StipulateFSharpChecks, which changes how F# compiles.
    -->
    <CoreCompileCache Include="StipulateMode=$(StipulateMode)" />
    <CoreCompileCache Include="StipulateFSharpChecks=$(StipulateFSharpChecks)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Interceptors -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(StipulateMode)' == 'Interceptors'">
    <InterceptorsNamespaces>$(InterceptorsNamespaces);Net.NowhereAtAll.Stipulate.Generated</InterceptorsNamespaces>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Once NuGet has added the package's analyzers, take out the ones this project shouldn't have.
    NuGet adds them to every project that gets the package, directly or not (even through a
    package whose dependency on Stipulate excludes them, as a PackageReference's defaults do).

    A project left out by StipulateTransitive=false, one that doesn't reference Stipulate itself,
    gets none of them; nor does a project nothing can be enforced in (see StipulateReportLanguage):
    one in a language other than C#, Visual Basic and F#, or a Visual Basic or F# one in
    Interceptors mode.

    The package ships the generator with the analyzers, so the compiler would load it in every
    mode; outside Interceptors mode, take it out again.

    A project that enforces the attributes, by weaving or with interceptors, compiles in the check
    they both call, Stipulate.ArgumentChecks.cs (or .vb, or .fs): as source, so that enforcing them
    adds no dependency at run time. F# compiles its files in order, so there the check comes first.
  -->
  <Target Name="StipulateResolveAnalyzers" AfterTargets="ResolveLockFileAnalyzers">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_StipulateDirectReference Include="@(PackageReference)" Condition="'%(Identity)' == 'Stipulate'" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_StipulateCheckSource Condition="'$(Language)' == 'C#'">Stipulate.ArgumentChecks.cs</_StipulateCheckSource>
      <_StipulateCheckSource Condition="'$(Language)' == 'VB'">Stipulate.ArgumentChecks.vb</_StipulateCheckSource>
      <_StipulateCheckSource Condition="'$(Language)' == 'F#'">Stipulate.ArgumentChecks.fs</_StipulateCheckSource>
      <_StipulateUnenforceable Condition="'$(_StipulateCheckSource)' == ''">true</_StipulateUnenforceable>
      <_StipulateUnenforceable Condition="'$(Language)' != 'C#' And '$(StipulateMode)' == 'Interceptors'">true</_StipulateUnenforceable>
      <_StipulateLeftOut Condition="'$(StipulateTransitive)' == 'false' And '@(_StipulateDirectReference)' == ''">true</_StipulateLeftOut>
      <_StipulateReportsLanguage Condition="'$(_StipulateLeftOut)' != 'true' And '$(_StipulateUnenforceable)' == 'true' And '$(StipulateMode)' != 'None'">true</_StipulateReportsLanguage>
      <_StipulateWeavesFSharp Condition="'$(_StipulateLeftOut)' != 'true' And '$(Language)' == 'F#' And '$(StipulateMode)' == 'Weaving'">true</_StipulateWeavesFSharp>
      <_StipulateLeftOut Condition="'$(_StipulateUnenforceable)' == 'true'">true</_StipulateLeftOut>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(_StipulateLeftOut)' == 'true'">
      <Analyzer Remove="@(Analyzer)" Condition="'%(Analyzer.NuGetPackageId)' == 'Stipulate'" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(StipulateMode)' != 'Interceptors'">
      <Analyzer Remove="@(Analyzer)" Condition="'%(Analyzer.Filename)' == 'Net.NowhereAtAll.Stipulate.Generator'" />
    </ItemGroup>
    <ItemGroup Condition="'$(_StipulateLeftOut)' != 'true' And ('$(StipulateMode)' == 'Weaving' Or '$(StipulateMode)' == 'Interceptors')">
      <_StipulateCheck Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)$(_StipulateCheckSource)" />
      <!--
        Once, even if the project's own globs took it in (packages restored inside the project's
        folder), as a relative path: Visual Basic rejects a file compiled twice.
      -->
      <Compile Remove="@(Compile)" Condition="'%(Compile.FullPath)' == '@(_StipulateCheck->'%(FullPath)')'" />
      <_StipulateProjectSources Include="@(Compile)" Condition="'$(Language)' == 'F#'" />
      <Compile Remove="@(_StipulateProjectSources)" />
      <Compile Include="@(_StipulateCheck);@(_StipulateProjectSources)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    StipulateFSharpChecks, in an F# project that is woven: see the top of this file. F# reads
    OtherFlags when it compiles, after this has added to it.
  -->
  <Target Name="StipulateFSharpChecks"
          AfterTargets="StipulateResolveAnalyzers"
          Condition="'$(_StipulateWeavesFSharp)' == 'true'">
    <Error Condition="'$(StipulateFSharpChecks)' != 'EveryCall' And '$(StipulateFSharpChecks)' != 'CallsFromOtherAssemblies' And '$(StipulateFSharpChecks)' != 'NoInliningMethods'"
           Code="ST0009"
           Text="StipulateFSharpChecks is '$(StipulateFSharpChecks)', which isn't one of its values, so Stipulate can't tell which calls to check in '$(MSBuildProjectName)'. Set it to EveryCall (the default: every call is checked, and F#'s optimizer inlines nothing), CallsFromOtherAssemblies (calls from other assemblies are checked; within the project, F#'s optimizer may inline a small method, unchecked), or NoInliningMethods (only methods marked [&lt;MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.NoInlining)&gt;] are certainly checked; F#'s optimizer works as usual)." />
    <PropertyGroup>
      <OtherFlags Condition="'$(StipulateFSharpChecks)' == 'EveryCall'">$(OtherFlags) --localoptimize- --nooptimizationdata</OtherFlags>
      <OtherFlags Condition="'$(StipulateFSharpChecks)' == 'CallsFromOtherAssemblies'">$(OtherFlags) --nooptimizationdata</OtherFlags>
    </PropertyGroup>
  </Target>

  <!--
    Say that a project StipulateResolveAnalyzers left out because nothing can be enforced in it
    isn't enforced, unless StipulateTransitive=false would have left it out anyway or its
    StipulateMode is None: once per build, not in a design-time build too.
  -->
  <Target Name="StipulateReportLanguage"
          AfterTargets="StipulateResolveAnalyzers"
          Condition="'$(_StipulateReportsLanguage)' == 'true' And '$(DesignTimeBuild)' != 'true'">
    <Warning Condition="'$(_StipulateCheckSource)' == ''"
             Code="ST0008"
             Text="Stipulate enforces validation attributes only in C#, Visual Basic and F# projects, and '$(MSBuildProjectName)' is $(Language): the attributes on its parameters aren't checked when its methods are called, and its implementations aren't checked against their contracts. What it calls in assemblies that Stipulate wove is still checked. To have its methods checked, move them into a project in one of those languages; to acknowledge that they aren't, set StipulateMode to None in this project." />
    <Warning Condition="'$(_StipulateCheckSource)' != ''"
             Code="ST0008"
             Text="Stipulate's interceptors are a C# feature, and '$(MSBuildProjectName)' is $(Language): in Interceptors mode, the attributes on its parameters aren't checked when its methods are called. What it calls in assemblies that Stipulate wove is still checked. To have its methods checked, set StipulateMode to Weaving in this project; to acknowledge that they aren't, set it to None." />
  </Target>

  <!-- Weaving -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(StipulateMode)' == 'Weaving'">
    <StipulateWeave>true</StipulateWeave>
    <StipulateWeaverPath Condition="'$(StipulateWeaverPath)' == ''">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\tools\net8.0\Net.NowhereAtAll.Stipulate.Weaver.dll</StipulateWeaverPath>
    <StipulateReferencesFile>$(IntermediateOutputPath)Stipulate.references</StipulateReferencesFile>
    <StipulateDotnet>$(DOTNET_HOST_PATH)</StipulateDotnet>
    <StipulateDotnet Condition="'$(StipulateDotnet)' == ''">dotnet</StipulateDotnet>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Recompile (and so re-weave) when the weaver itself changes. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(StipulateWeave)' == 'true'">
    <CustomAdditionalCompileInputs Include="$(StipulateWeaverPath)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Rewrite the freshly compiled assembly in obj\ before it is copied to bin\. Runs even when
    CoreCompile was skipped; the weaver recognises an already-woven assembly and leaves it alone.
    Not in a project StipulateTransitive=false leaves out (see StipulateResolveAnalyzers), nor in
    an IDE's design-time build, which runs CoreCompile only for the compiler's arguments: nothing
    is compiled there, and the weaver may not even be built yet.
  -->
  <Target Name="StipulateWeave"
          AfterTargets="CoreCompile"
          Condition="'$(StipulateWeave)' == 'true' And '$(_StipulateLeftOut)' != 'true' And '$(DesignTimeBuild)' != 'true' And '$(SkipCompilerExecution)' != 'true'">
    <Error Condition="!Exists('$(StipulateWeaverPath)')"
           Code="ST0005"
           Text="The Stipulate weaver isn't where StipulateWeaverPath says: '$(StipulateWeaverPath)' does not exist." />
    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(StipulateReferencesFile)"
                      Lines="@(ReferencePath->'%(FullPath)')"
                      Overwrite="true"
                      WriteOnlyWhenDifferent="true" />
    <!--
      The weaver reports its own failures as canonical "error STxxxx:" lines (which Exec turns into
      build errors) and then exits with 2. Any other non-zero exit means it crashed or never
      started, without reporting anything, so that gets a specific error instead of MSB3073.
    -->
    <Exec Command="&quot;$(StipulateDotnet)&quot; &quot;$(StipulateWeaverPath)&quot; &quot;@(IntermediateAssembly->'%(FullPath)')&quot; &quot;$(StipulateReferencesFile)&quot;"
          IgnoreExitCode="true">
      <Output TaskParameter="ExitCode" PropertyName="_StipulateExitCode" />
    </Exec>
    <Error Condition="'$(_StipulateExitCode)' != '0' And '$(_StipulateExitCode)' != '2'"
           Code="ST0004"
           Text="The Stipulate weaver crashed before it could weave '@(IntermediateAssembly)' (exit code $(_StipulateExitCode)); see the build output above for the exception. On Windows, 'An Application Control policy has blocked this file' means Windows refused to load the weaver; build under WSL or ask for an exception for this folder." />
  </Target>

</Project>
